# Who has to review what.
#
# `verification/` is the only place a publisher tier comes from, and the tier is the one claim in
# this registry that a person would act on: `verified` says somebody here checked who a publisher is.
# A record written by the publisher it is about would be a claim wearing a check's clothes, so this
# path needs a maintainer's review, and the validator separately refuses a change to it that does not
# carry the maintainer-only `verification` label.
#
# **Two guards on one door, and they are enforced in different places.** The label is what CI can see
# and fail on. This file only does anything with branch protection set to require review from code
# owners — which is a repository setting rather than something in the tree, so it is worth checking
# rather than assuming. See docs/verification.md.
/verification/  @Redth

# `curation/` is the shop window, and it is here for a related reason rather than the same one. A
# verification is a claim that had to survive a check; featuring survives no check, because there is
# nothing to check — it is an opinion, and an opinion published beside somebody else's binary is
# still ours. It is also the field a submitter would most like to write, which is why there is no
# field and why this path needs the maintainer-only `curation` label as well as a review here.
/curation/      @Redth

# The rules themselves, and the trust statement that goes with them.
/tools/registry/  @Redth
/schema/          @Redth
/keys/            @Redth
/.github/         @Redth
